Beryllium: the trap switch reports each refused call with its stack, and the last refused call is answered before the lockdown

Purpose: plan/18 section 3.2, P2 (feature matrix S-REN-02). Every survey
  since 0.20.3.3 shows one call refused after a renderer's lockdown, about
  once a renderer: cpuset_getaffinity. It does no harm. It has to go
  because nothing else is refused, and a renderer that is refused nothing
  can be run with the kernel's capability trap as a tripwire.
  - Where the call comes from, read from the sources: FreeBSD's
    sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN) asks for the root CPU set first
    (cpuset_getaffinity with CPU_LEVEL_ROOT; capability mode allows only a
    process's or thread's own set) and then answers from the ELF auxiliary
    vector. The bundled libc++ implements
    std::thread::hardware_concurrency() with that sysconf. abseil's
    NumCPUs() calls it once per process, the first time one of its locks
    is contended, and keeps the answer. V8 has the same sysconf in a
    function Chromium does not call, and Chromium's own processor count
    reads hw.ncpu, which capability mode allows.
  - sandbox/policy/freebsd/sandbox_freebsd.cc, WarmUpForCapabilityMode():
    absl::base_internal::NumCPUs() is called before the lockdown, so the
    answer is kept by then. That abseil is the caller is an inference from
    the count (at most one a renderer); the next point is how to know.
  - BerylliumCapsicumTrap (PROC_TRAPCAP_CTL) made the kernel raise SIGTRAP
    on a refused call, nothing handled it, and the renderer ended at its
    first one. Now a handler writes one line for each refused call and
    returns, so the call fails as it would without the switch:
      beryllium-sandbox: renderer pid=N refused in capability mode:
        syscall=S errno=E thread=T frames: module+0xoffset ...
    The first frame is where the call was made, the rest are the return
    addresses from the frame pointers, in the form the socket pair lines
    use, which the test suite names with llvm-symbolizer. A run with the
    switch lists everything a confined renderer still reaches for, each
    with the code that reached.
  The handler runs in the thread that made the call, possibly inside a
    library holding a lock, so it takes none: no logging, no allocation,
    no dladdr(). The modules are listed once before the lockdown (a module
    loaded later shows as a bare address), numbers are formatted by hand,
    and the line goes to stderr with write(2). At most 200 lines a process.
    A SIGTRAP that is not the kernel's capability trap (a failed CHECK ends
    in one) is passed to the handler that was installed before, or ends
    the process as before.
Stage: 4 (Beryllium series). Depends on beryllium-capsicum-renderer.patch
  and, for the place in the file, beryllium-renderer-hardening.patch.
Refreshed against: Chromium 156.0.8078.79, Helium 0.20.3 (2f81b9ca),
  FreeBSD ports 6ad3e0c8, using freebsd/tools/patch-workbench. Not yet
  compiled (written for build 0.20.3.22). The lockdown's own code, taken
  from the patched file, type-checks against FreeBSD 16's headers with
  stand-ins for Chromium's declarations. The handler and the module list,
  taken from the patched file and run on Linux with a breakpoint standing
  in for the kernel's trap, wrote the line with the trapping function and
  its callers named correctly, and the program went on.
Test: freebsd/tests/browser/tests/test_50_sandbox.py, class TrapReport: a
  browser with the lockdown and BerylliumCapsicumTrap; under the gate on a
  build that reports, no renderer may write a "refused in capability mode"
  line, and each line found is shown with its frames named. The Capsicum
  survey's after-lockdown list says the same from the kernel's side.
security-review: required (sandbox setup, signal handling in a confined
  process)

